claims.evidence:
Lines 4-6: function invoiceNote(note) {
  return `<div class="note">${note}</div>`;
}
The `note` parameter is interpolated directly into an HTML string without escaping. If this HTML is sent to a browser in a response, any HTML/JavaScript in `note` executes.

Lines 4-8: function currentUser(req) {
  const bearer = (req.headers.authorization || '').replace('Bearer ', '');
  const claims = jwt.decode(bearer);
  return { id: claims && claims.sub, role: claims && claims.role };
}
The code calls jwt.decode() which only decodes the JWT payload without verifying the signature. Any JWT can be forged by an attacker.

Line 24: const order = req.query.order || 'created_at';
         db.query(`SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE owner_id = $1 ORDER BY ${order}`, [req.user.id])
The `order` parameter from the query string is interpolated directly into the SQL ORDER BY clause without validation or parameterization.

Lines 5-8: async function fetchLogo(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url, { redirect: 'follow' });
  return Buffer.from(await response.arrayBuffer());
}
Called from invoices.js line 38: const image = await fetchLogo(req.body.logoUrl);
The URL comes directly from req.body.logoUrl with no validation, and redirect: 'follow' allows following redirect chains.

Lines 7-10: router.get('/invoices/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const invoice = await db.query('SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE id = $1', [req.params.id]);
  res.json(invoice);
});
The query filters only by invoice id, not by owner. Contrast with line 14-17 (/receipts/:id) which includes AND owner_id = $2.
